<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">European Free Trade Association</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1960">1960</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as EFTA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The European Free Trade Association is a free-trade organisation founded in 1960 by seven European states outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>. Most founders later joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, and its members are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, the first three of which belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-area">European Economic Area</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/european-free-trade-association.jpg" alt="European Free Trade Association" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EFTA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EFTA_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The European Free Trade Association was set up in 1960 as an alternative trade bloc for European states that were unable or unwilling to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>. The convention was drawn up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> and provided for the progressive removal of customs duties on industrial products, leaving agricultural and fisheries products aside. Seven states founded it: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the United Kingdom, often called the Outer Seven in the 1960s, in contrast to the Inner Six of the Community.</p><p>The main difference from the early Community was that it had no common external customs tariff. Each member remained free to set its own duties on goods from outside the association and to make its own free trade agreements with other countries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Membership changed as members left for the Community. Finland became an associate member in 1961 and a full member in 1986, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> joined in 1970. The United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> left in 1973 on joining the Community, and Portugal left to join it in the 1980s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a> joined in 1991; until then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> had represented its interests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and Finland joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 1995, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and Switzerland of the founders. Norway's governments twice sought to join, but referendums rejected membership in 1972 and in 1994. Switzerland rejected membership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-area">European Economic Area</a> in a referendum in 1992.</p><p>The Agreement on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-area">European Economic Area</a> was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> in 1992 and took effect on 1 January 1994. It lets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> take part in the internal market of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> without belonging to it, applying almost all its market legislation except on agriculture and fisheries. Because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/court-of-justice-of-the-european-union">European Court of Justice</a> ruled in Opinion 1/91 that the EU's institutions could not hold such powers over non-members, the EFTA Surveillance Authority and the EFTA Court were created for this purpose. The Portugal Fund, a low-interest loan of US$100 million from the members, began in February 1977 to support Portugal's development after the Carnation Revolution and was dissolved in January 2002.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The association is not a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a>, though it has a coordinated trade policy, and its members have jointly concluded free trade agreements with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and with many other countries. A revised treaty, the Vaduz Convention, was signed on 21 June 2001 and took effect on 1 June 2002. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, outside the Economic Area, has instead a set of bilateral agreements with the European Union. Citizens of its member states can live and work in the other member states. The association also originated the Hallmarking Convention and the Pharmaceutical Inspection Convention, both open to other states.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “European Free Trade Association” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liechtenstein joined the Council of Europe in 1978, the United Nations in 1990, the <b>European Free Trade Association</b> as a full member in 1991 and the European Economic Area in 1995, and it became part of the Schengen area in 2011.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
